ASSIGNMENT AND ASSUMPTION AGREEMENT


 This ASSIGNMENT AND ASSUMPTION AGREEMENT (this “Agreement”) is entered into as of ______________, _____, by and among ENRON POWER MARKETING, INC., a Delaware corporation (“Seller”), __________________________________________, a __________________________________ (“Buyer,” and with Seller, each called a “Party”), and for purposes of Section 1.1 only, TENNESSEE VALLEY AUTHORITY, a corporate instrumentality and agency of the United States of America (“Assignor”).

 For and in consideration of the provisions set forth below, the Parties and (with respect to Section 1.1 only) Assignor, intending to be legally bound, agree as follows:


ARTICLE I
OPTION

 1.1 Assignment of Options.  Assignor owns certain options granted by Seller to purchase firm seasonal peaking electric energy.  For good and valuable consideration, by this Agreement Assignor assigns and transfers to Buyer, and Buyer accepts and assumes, all rights and obligations of Assignor under the options (each an “OPA”) as provided below during the periods and on the terms set forth in this Agreement.  Seller consents to this assignment and assumption and releases Assignor from all obligations relating to the OPAs (as defined in this Agreement) arising from and after the date of this Agreement.  Seller, Buyer, and Assignor acknowledge that certain of the OPAs (as defined in this Agreement) until this  assignment and assumption may have formed a part of options held by Assignor, that in any such case only those parts constituting OPAs (as defined in this Agreement) have been assigned to and assumed by Buyer, and that Assignor is not released as to the parts (if any) of those options not so assigned or assumed or as to any other options under existing arrangements between Seller and Assignor.

 1.2 Description of OPAs.  Each OPA has the following features:

  (a) a “Number,” which is set forth on Exhibit 1.2;

  (b) a “Strike Date,” which is set forth on Exhibit 1.2, or if that OPA has been exercised, the notation “Exercised”;

  (c) a period during which deliveries of energy may be made (the “Seasonal Delivery Period”), which commences immediately after midnight Central Standard Time or Central Daylight Saving Time, whichever is prevailing as most recently notified by Buyer to Seller (“Central Time”), on the day before the date appearing under “Delivery Start Date” on Exhibit 1.2 and ends at midnight Central Time on the date appearing under “Delivery End Date” on Exhibit 1.2;

  (d) a “Demand Charge” per kilowatt-month and an “Energy Charge” per megawatt-hour, each of which is set forth on Exhibit 1.2;

  (e) an “OPA Quantity,” which is set forth on Exhibit 1.2; and

  (f) a “Maximum Contract Quantity,” which is set forth on Exhibit 1.2.

 1.3 Exercise of OPAs.  Each of the OPAs identified on Exhibit 1.2 as “Exercised,” and each OPA exercised in accordance with this Section 1.3 is called an “Exercised OPA.”  To exercise an OPA that is not identified on Exhibit 1.2 as an Exercised OPA, Buyer must notify Seller before midnight Central Time on the Strike Date for that OPA.  The notice must be substantially in the form of Exhibit 1.3 and must include the information required in that Exhibit.  Once an OPA is exercised, it is an Exercised OPA.  On any such exercise, Seller shall become obligated to sell and deliver, and Buyer shall become obligated to purchase, take delivery of, and pay for, energy as provided in this Agreement.  If an OPA is not exercised in accordance with this Section 1.3 on or before its Strike Date, that OPA lapses, and neither Party has any further rights or obligations with respect to that OPA.

 1.4 Interpretation.  Unless otherwise expressly stated, references in this Agreement to “Articles” and “Sections” are to Articles and Sections of this Agreement, and references to “Exhibits” are to the Exhibits attached to this Agreement.  Words defined in the singular have the corresponding meaning in the plural and vice versa.  Use of “including” means including without limitation.  References to one gender include all others.  References to “MW” and “KW” are to megawatts (1,000,000 watts) and kilowatts (1,000 watts), respectively, and references to “MWH” and “KWH” are to megawatt-hours and kilowatt-hours, respectively.  References to “Days” are to the period from immediately after midnight through the next succeeding midnight, Central Time, and to “Business Days” are to Days other than Saturdays, Sundays, and federal holidays.

