ARTICLE 2
CHARGES, QUANTITY, AND SCHEDULING

 2.1 Obligation to Deliver and Take.  Subject to the other provisions of this Agreement:

  (a) Buyer shall pay the Demand Charge for the Seasonal Delivery Period for each Exercised OPA, and

  (b) for each period of time for which energy is scheduled in accordance with Section 2.2 (a “Scheduled Period”), Seller shall sell and deliver the amount of energy scheduled in accordance with Section 2.2 for that Scheduled Period (the “Contract Quantity”), and Buyer shall purchase, take delivery of, and pay the Energy Charge for the energy so delivered.

 2.2 Scheduling. (a) Buyer may request, in accordance with this Section 2.2, scheduling of deliveries of energy, during any hours of the Seasonal Delivery Period for which there is an Exercised OPA, at an instantaneous rate equal to an integral multiple (up to the total number of Exercised OPAs for that Seasonal Delivery Period) of the OPA Quantity for a single OPA, all in accordance with the provisions of this Section 2.2; provided, however, that Buyer may not request scheduling of energy during any period of time to the extent that the energy requested, if delivered, would cause the total amount of energy scheduled during any Seasonal Delivery Period to exceed the sum of Maximum Contract Quantities for all OPAs for that Seasonal Delivery Period that are Exercised OPAs.

 (b) Buyer is not required to request and schedule any minimum quantity of energy on account of any Exercised OPA.

 (c) Except as the Parties may agree otherwise in writing, deliveries of energy for any Seasonal Delivery Period may be scheduled only at a rate equal to an integral multiple (up to the total number of Exercised OPAs for that Seasonal Delivery Period) of the OPA Quantity for a single OPA and in increments of eight continuous hours beginning and ending on a quarter hour and wholly within one Day; provided, however, that if scheduling for a full eight hours would cause the total amount of energy scheduled during that Seasonal Delivery Period to exceed the sum of the Maximum Contract Quantities for all Exercised OPAs for that Seasonal Delivery Period, the minimum increment shall be shortened to the extent required to cause the total amount of energy scheduled during that Seasonal Delivery Period to equal the sum of the Maximum Contract Quantities for all Exercised OPAs for that Seasonal Delivery Period.  Each such increment nonetheless must be wholly within the Seasonal Delivery Period for those Exercised OPAs.

 (d) Buyer shall request the scheduling of energy by informing Seller of:

  (i) the rate at which scheduling is requested, and

  (ii) the requested Scheduled Period, 

all of which must comply with the other provisions of this Section 2.2, by telephone call to (800) 349-5527, confirmed by facsimile transmission to (713) 646-8272.  Seller promptly shall notify Buyer of any change in these telephone and facsimile numbers.  Any facsimile or other written communication shall be deemed to have been made when Seller actually receives it.  Seller shall be entitled to rely on any such communication as being genuine that Seller’s personnel in good faith believe to be genuine.  Buyer must request the scheduling of deliveries of energy for any Day at or before 1000 Central Time the Business Day before.  All amounts so requested and complying with the other provisions of this Article 2 shall be deemed to be scheduled.

 (e) Without limiting the other provisions of this Agreement, after the delivery of energy has been scheduled for any Scheduled Period in accordance with the preceding provisions of this Section 2.2, Seller may discontinue delivery of all or a portion of the Contract Quantity for the Scheduled Period without being in breach of this Agreement, and Buyer may discontinue taking all or a portion of the Contract Quantity for the Scheduled Period without being in breach of this Agreement, provided the Parties agree, by telephone call confirmed as provided in Section 2.2(d), prior to the interruption in delivery or taking delivery, which agreement shall include:

  (i) a statement of the interruption premium, if any, being paid by the Party desiring to be relieved of obligations to supply to, or take deliveries from, the other Party,

  (ii) the starting and the ending time of the interruption, and

  (iii) any other terms and conditions the Parties may deem appropriate.

Any such interruption (an “Agreed Interruption”) shall not be considered to be a default or a breach of this Agreement.

 (f) Each Party shall maintain a written log indicating each Scheduled Period, the Contract Quantity scheduled for that Scheduled Period, and actual quantities delivered or taken during that Scheduled Period.


ARTICLE 3
FAILURE TO DELIVER OR TAKE

 3.1 Buyer Failure.  If Buyer fails to take delivery of all or part of the Contract Quantity for any Scheduled Period (unless the failure is excused under Article 10, is an Agreed Interruption, or is caused by Seller) or if Buyer notifies Seller under Section 4.1(e) that it will not take those deliveries, then Buyer shall pay Seller, on the date payments for the month in which that Scheduled Period occurs are due to Seller under Article 8, in addition to the payments due under Article 8, an amount equal to:

  (a) the amount of energy not so taken, expressed in MWH,

multiplied by

  (b) the excess, if any, of the Energy Charge for OPAs for that Scheduled Period over the price (expressed in $/MWH) at which Seller was, or using commercially reasonable efforts should have been, able to sell or otherwise dispose of the energy not so taken at a commercially reasonable price (adjusted to reflect differences in transmission costs, if any).

 3.2 Seller Failure.  (a) If Seller fails to deliver all or part of the Contract Quantity for any Scheduled Period (unless the failure is excused under Article 10, is an Agreed Interruption, or is caused by Buyer), then Seller shall pay Buyer, on the date payments for the month in which that Scheduled Period occurs are due to Seller under Article 8, an amount equal to:

  (i) the amount of energy Seller so failed to deliver, expressed in MWH,

multiplied by

  (ii) the excess, if any, of the price (expressed in $/MWH) at which Buyer was, or using commercially reasonable efforts should have been, able to purchase or otherwise acquire (including, if applicable, the cost of generating the energy itself) the energy Seller so failed to deliver at a commercially reasonable price (adjusted to reflect differences in transmission costs, if any) over the Energy Charge for OPAs for that Scheduled Period;

provided, however, that to the extent Buyer is unable, after commercially reasonable efforts, to replace the undelivered energy at a reasonable cost, whether by purchase or generation at its own facilities, Seller instead shall pay Buyer an amount equal to the loss in Buyer’s margin associated with the curtailment of Buyer’s system load (meaning revenues that would have been received on account of the curtailed load less the energy charges that would have been due to Seller had the energy been delivered) not to exceed $60.00 per MWH of curtailed load.

 (b) Buyer acknowledges that, from time to time, circumstances not constituting force majeure (as defined in Article 10) may arise that temporarily prevent Seller from delivering energy continuously during Scheduled Periods at the rate at which the delivery of energy is scheduled in accordance with Section 2.2.  These circumstances can include failure of firm supply or transmission not constituting force majeure and interruption of nonfirm supply or transmission.  Should any of these circumstances arise, Seller shall not be deemed to be in breach of this Agreement to the extent that:

  (i) the interruption lasts longer than 10 continuous minutes;

  (ii) Seller promptly notifies Buyer of the nature of the circumstances that have occurred, including whether the interruption is a Seller Firm Temporary Interruption or a Seller Nonfirm Temporary Interruption; and

  (iii) Seller is using commercially reasonable efforts to eliminate the circumstances, to obtain supply or transmission from other sources, or (in conjunction with Buyer) to reconfigure the Scheduled Delivery Point(s), in each case so as to resume as soon as practicable the delivery of energy at the rate at which the delivery of energy has been scheduled in accordance with Section 2.2.

Any such interruption is called a “Seller Firm Temporary Interruption” if the interruption is caused by the interruption of firm supply or transmission and otherwise is called a “Seller Nonfirm Temporary Interruption.”  Seller nonetheless shall be liable for payments under Section 3.2(a) for any energy not delivered on account of the reduction in the rate of delivery of energy during a Seller Firm Temporary Interruption or a Seller Nonfirm Temporary Interruption.  In any case, an interruption in Seller’s deliveries of energy as a result of loss of firm or nonfirm supply or transmission lasting 10 continuous minutes or less that Seller is able to remedy within that time period shall not be considered to be a breach of Seller’s obligations under this Agreement for any purpose, including with respect to payments due under Section 3.2(a) or (c) or Buyer’s rights under Section 6.4.

 (c) If Seller fails during any Scheduled Period to deliver energy at the rate at which the delivery of energy has been scheduled in accordance with Section 2.2 (unless the failure is excused under Article 10, is an Agreed Interruption, is caused by Buyer, or is a Seller Firm Temporary Interruption, with the energy not so delivered due to the circumstances described within these parentheses being called “Excluded Deliveries”), then the aggregate amount due on account of the Demand Charges for all Exercised OPAs for the month in which that Scheduled Period occurs shall be reduced by the result of the following equation:

       DC1       
      ─────   ×   DRmax
        30

where:

 DC1 = the Demand Charge established in Exhibit 1.2 (converted to dollars per megawatt-month by multiplying the amount on Exhibit 1.2 by 1,000) for a single megawatt-month for the Seasonal Delivery Period in which that Day occurs

 DRmax = the Deficiency Rate (expressed in MW) for whichever Scheduled Hour during a Scheduled Period during that Day has the highest Deficiency Rate.  The “Deficiency Rate” for any Scheduled Hour is the result of the following equation:

       RH   +   RL
     RS   −    ──────────
              2

   where:

    RS = the rate at which the delivery of energy is scheduled during that Scheduled Hour in accordance with Section 2.2

    RH = the highest instantaneous rate of delivery of energy (expressed in MW) during that Scheduled Hour, but calculated assuming that Excluded Deliveries had been delivered

    RL = the lowest instantaneous rate of delivery of energy (expressed in MW) during that Scheduled Hour, but calculated assuming that Excluded Deliveries had been delivered

For purposes of this calculation, “Scheduled Hour” means, during any Scheduled Period, any of the consecutive 60-minute periods during a Scheduled Period beginning and ending on the same quarter- hour as the applicable Scheduled Period began.  If more than one such Day occurs during a Seasonal Delivery Period, the reduction shall be made for each such Day, but if the circumstances occur with respect to more than one Scheduled Period during a single Day, only one reduction in the Demand Charge shall be made for that Deficiency Day, which shall be the highest such reduction.  In no circumstances, however, shall the amount in Section 8.1(a) be reduced to below zero.

 3.3 Reasonableness; Exclusive Remedy.  (a) Both Parties stipulate that the payment obligations set forth in Sections 3.1 and 3.2 are reasonable in light of the anticipated harm and the difficulty of estimation or calculation of actual damages, and each Party waives the right to contest those payments as an unreasonable penalty.  Except as provided in Articles 6 and 8, the remedies set forth in this Article 3 are the sole and exclusive remedies of the aggrieved Party for the failure of the other Party to deliver or accept the full Contract Quantity, and each Party waives all rights to seek or collect all other damages.

 (b) The Parties confirm that the express remedies and measures of damages provided in this Agreement satisfy the essential purposes of this Agreement.  If no remedy or measure of damages is expressly provided in this Agreement, the obligor’s liability shall be limited to direct actual damages only, which direct actual damages shall be the sole and exclusive remedy and the other Party waives all other remedies or damages at law or in equity.  Neither Party shall be liable for consequential, incidental, punitive, exemplary, or indirect damages, in tort, contract, under any indemnity provision or otherwise.  It is the intent of the Parties that the limitations imposed by this Section 3.3 on remedies and the measure of damages be without regard to the cause or causes related to the damages, including, without limitation, the negligence of any Party, whether negligence be sole, joint, or concurrent, or active or passive.  To the extent any damages required to be paid under this Agreement are liquidated, the Parties acknowledge that the damages are difficult or impossible to determine, otherwise obtaining an adequate remedy is inconvenient and the liquidated damages constitute a reasonable approximation of the harm or loss.

 (c) Buyer acknowledges that it has entered into this Agreement and is contracting for the energy to be supplied by Seller based solely on the express representations and warranties set forth in this Agreement.  Seller expressly negates any other representation or warranty, written or oral, express or implied.

 (d) The Parties acknowledge the duty to mitigate damages under this Agreement, and the Parties will maintain records regarding their damages or recoveries under this Article 3 and allow access to those records as provided in Section 8.2(e).  In this connection, the Parties recognize that the ability to effectuate arrangements for the sale or purchase of energy is conditioned upon the volatility of energy markets, the creditworthiness and reliability of potential customers, the complexity and size of the portfolios of contracts managed by each Party and the need to conduct market business in an orderly manner.  In the case of Section 6.1, the Parties agree that 30 days after the end of the month in which the Early Termination Date occurs is a commercially reasonable period after the establishment of an Early Termination Date to determine the Termination Payment; provided, however, that notwithstanding the foregoing, the Parties recognize that a longer period may ordinarily be required to determine the Termination Payment in an orderly manner so as not to adversely affect the energy market.  Each Party may utilize its discretion, with commercially reasonable foresight, to adjust the timing and staggering of the purchases or sales of energy volumes in its efforts to mitigate damages.

ARTICLE 4
DELIVERY

 4.1 Delivery Points.  (a)  Seller shall deliver scheduled energy by making it available at, and Buyer shall receive and accept scheduled energy at, the Scheduled Delivery Point(s) designated in accordance with this Section 4.1.  For purposes of this Agreement, “Permitted Delivery Point” means an interface between Assignor’s system and the system of another electric utility (an “Interconnecting Utility”), including the interconnections listed on Exhibit 4.1(a).

 (b) At or before 1200 Central Time on the Thursday immediately preceding each week or partial week during the Seasonal Delivery Period for an Exercised OPA, Seller shall notify Buyer of  the  Permitted Delivery Points that Seller then would expect to designate as the interfaces at which deliveries of energy scheduled during that week in accordance with Section 2.2 would be made and the expected quantities at those Permitted Delivery Points.

   (c) At or before 1200 Central Time on the Business Day before the Day on which the energy is to be delivered, Seller shall provide Buyer with a confirmation, substantially in the form of Exhibit 4.1(c), that designates:

  (i) each Permitted Delivery Point at which Seller will deliver all or a portion of the energy scheduled to be delivered during that Day (each a “Scheduled Delivery Point,” as it may be modified in accordance with this Agreement, including if applicable any location designated in accordance with Section 4.1(d) that is not a Permitted Delivery Point); and

  (ii) the instantaneous rate of delivery (expressed in MW) for the energy scheduled to be delivered at each Scheduled Delivery Point during each period during that Day for which the delivery of energy is to be made at that Scheduled Delivery Point.

The instantaneous rate of delivery of energy at any time at any Scheduled Delivery Point as designated in the confirmation may exceed the amount set forth for that Scheduled Delivery Point on Exhibit 4.1(a) only with the consent of Buyer.  The aggregate instantaneous rates of delivery of energy scheduled for all Scheduled Delivery Points so selected at all times must equal the rate at which energy is scheduled to be delivered at that time in accordance with Section 2.2.

 (d) At or before 1400 Central Time on the Business Day before the Day on which the energy is to be delivered, Seller (if Buyer so requests) shall provide to Buyer, with respect to all or any portion of the energy to be delivered at any Scheduled Delivery Point as specified in the confirmation provided under Section 4.1(c):

  (i) the price for transmission of the energy from the Interconnecting Utility associated with the Scheduled Delivery Point specified in the confirmation provided under Section 4.1(c)  to another location (which need not be a Permitted Delivery Point) that Buyer requests Seller to arrange on Buyer’s behalf;

  (ii) the price per MWH at which Seller would be willing to sell the energy at another location (which need not be a Permitted Delivery Point) rather than the Scheduled Delivery Point specified in the confirmation provided under Section 4.1(c), which price may be no greater than the lowest price at which Seller then is offering to sell next-Day firm energy to third parties in the same quantity, for the same period, and at the same location as Buyer then is requesting; and/or

  (iii) the location(s) in the transmission path from Seller’s source to (but before) the Scheduled Delivery Point (including at the point at which the Interconnecting Utility would receive the energy)  at which Seller would be able to deliver the energy to Buyer or to another person arranged by Buyer.

Seller shall respond to any such request by telephone promptly (but no later than 1500 Central Time on the Business Day before the Day on which the energy is to be delivered) and shall indicate how long  (which must be as long as is commercially reasonable at the time) Buyer has to agree to any such alternative prices or locations.  To the extent that, within the time Seller so specified, Buyer informs Seller, by telephone call confirmed as provided in Section 2.2(d), that it wishes to accept any such alternative prices or locations:

  (A) Seller shall enter into all contracts and commitments with respect to any transmission that Seller arranges as described in clause (i) above and Buyer shall pay Seller, in addition to amounts otherwise due, a supplemental charge equal to the price agreed for transmission to the other location, and in that event the Scheduled Delivery Point shall be the point at which the Interconnecting Utility associated with the Scheduled Delivery Point established in the confirmation provided under Section 4.1(c) takes delivery of the energy (it being understood that Seller’s arrangement for transmission from that Scheduled Delivery Point to the other location as described in clause (i) above is as agent for Buyer and, accordingly, Buyer will indemnify Seller for all losses, costs, liabilities, and expenses associated with that transmission); and

  (B) with respect to any energy to be delivered at a location other than the Scheduled Delivery Point specified in the confirmation provided under Section 4.1(c) as described in clause (ii) above, Buyer shall pay Seller, in addition to amounts otherwise due, a supplemental energy charge for the energy delivered to the other location equal to:

   (x)  the excess (if any) of the agreed price per MWH of energy so delivered over the otherwise applicable Energy Charge,

 multiplied by

   (y) the quantity of energy delivered at that other location as opposed to the Scheduled Delivery Point designated in the confirmation provided under Section 4.1(c),

 and the Scheduled Delivery Points and the times and the rates of delivery shall be modified to take into account the changes in the periods, the rates, and the locations of delivery.

 (e) Buyer shall notify Seller at or before 1600 Central Time on the Day before the Day on which the energy is scheduled to be delivered if it will not be able to take delivery of all the energy scheduled to be delivered at any particular Scheduled Point of Delivery (taking into account any modifications entered into as provided in Section 2.2(e) or 4.1(d)) and, if so, the applicable times and rates of delivery.  If Buyer so notifies Seller, Seller shall not be obligated to deliver that energy, but the provisions of Section 3.1 shall apply and that quantity of energy shall be counted as having been scheduled and delivered for purposes of Sections 3.2(c) and 6.4 and the proviso at the end of Section 2.2(a).

 (f) Regardless of any arrangements entered into with the assistance of Seller as provided in Section 4.1(d), Buyer at its own expense may arrange for the transmission of energy scheduled to be delivered at a particular Scheduled Delivery Point in accordance with the preceding provisions of this Section 4.1 to another Permitted Delivery Point if, in Buyer’s sole judgment, that is appropriate or advisable in light of Buyer’s commitments to third parties or other factors.  Seller shall cooperate with Buyer in those arrangements; provided, however, that Seller is not obligated to incur any expenses or undertake any obligations to third parties.

 4.2 Title to Energy.  Title to the energy and risk of loss shall pass from Seller to Buyer at the Scheduled Delivery Point(s) once delivered.  As between the Parties, Seller shall be responsible for (and shall indemnify, defend and hold Buyer harmless from) any and all costs, fees, charges, expenses, or liabilities associated with the energy prior to delivery at the Scheduled Delivery Points and Buyer shall be responsible for (and shall indemnify, defend, and hold Seller harmless from) any and all costs, fees, charges, expenses, damages, or liabilities associated with the energy upon and after delivery at the Scheduled Delivery Points.

 4.3 Failures to Deliver or Accept.  (a) Each Party promptly shall notify the other of any interruption that it discovers in the delivery of energy scheduled in accordance with Section 2.2 at the rates and the locations determined under Section 4.1.

 (b) To the extent that energy scheduled in accordance with Section 2.2(d) for delivery at a Scheduled Delivery Point after application of all the provisions of Section 4.1 is received by the Interconnecting Utility with instructions to make it available to Buyer at that Scheduled Delivery Point, but the energy is not transferred at that Scheduled Delivery Point because the transfer capability at that Scheduled Delivery Point at the time of delivery is less than the amount specified for that Scheduled Delivery Point on Exhibit 4.1(a), subject to the provisions of Section 4.3(c) and (d) and Article 10, then Seller shall be deemed to have delivered that energy and Buyer shall be deemed to have failed to take delivery of that energy.

 (c) If at the time of scheduling or during a Scheduled Period, the transfer capability at a Scheduled Delivery Point is reduced due to:

  (i) one or more Seller transactions with other persons (except Assignor or other assignees of OPAs under the original agreement between Seller and Assignor, in each case on account of transactions involving OPAs under the original agreement with Assignor), or

  (ii) one or more transactions between other persons not parties to this Agreement (except Assignor or other assignees of OPAs under the original agreement between Seller and Assignor, in each case on account of transactions involving OPAs under the original agreement with Assignor), but only as to the transfer capability at the Scheduled Delivery Point above the amount set forth on Exhibit 4.1(a) that Buyer previously consented to permit Seller to use,

then to the extent the conditions in clause (i) or (ii) result in the need to change deliveries to other Permitted Delivery Points, Seller shall do so at a no additional cost to Buyer.

 (d) If at the time of scheduling or during a Scheduled Period, the transfer capability at a Scheduled Delivery Point is reduced due to:

  (i) one or more Buyer transactions with other persons (except Assignor or other assignees of OPAs under the original agreement between Seller and Assignor, in each case on account of transactions involving OPAs under the original agreement with Assignor), or

  (ii) one or more transactions between other persons not parties to this Agreement (except Assignor or other assignees of OPAs under the original agreement between Seller and Assignor, in each case on account of transactions involving OPAs under the original agreement with Assignor), but only as to the transfer capability at the Scheduled Delivery Point below the amount set forth on Exhibit 4.1(a),

then to the extent the conditions in clause (i) or (ii) result in the need to change deliveries to other Permitted Delivery Points, Buyer, at its election, either shall curtail its own transactions with others or shall select one or more of the alternatives provided in Section 4.1(d).

 4.4 Specifications. All energy to be delivered under this Agreement must be of a voltage, phase, frequency, and power factor substantially consistent with the other energy that is being transferred at the applicable Scheduled Delivery Point.

 4.5 Reliability.  The Parties shall carry out the scheduling and delivery of energy under this Agreement, to the extent within their respective control, without jeopardy to safe and reliable interconnected operation among Buyer and the Interconnecting Utilities.  If operating conditions or circumstances arise through no fault of Buyer that jeopardize this safe and reliable interconnected operation, the Parties recognize that Buyer and the Interconnecting Utilities shall take such actions, consistent with good utility practice, as they deem necessary to relieve the problems, which may include curtailment or reduction of deliveries of energy scheduled in accordance with Section 2.2 at Scheduled Delivery Points or other locations.  Any such action or need for action nonetheless does not excuse any consequent failure by a Party to perform its obligations under this Agreement, unless and to the extent that failure is excused under other provisions of this Agreement, including, to the extent applicable, Article 10.


ARTICLE 5
REPRESENTATIONS AND WARRANTIES

 Each Party, as a material inducement to the other Party’s entering into this Agreement, represents and warrants to the other Party that, as of the date of this Agreement and continuously from that time through the last Day of the last Seasonal Delivery Period for an Exercised OPA to end:

  (a) there are no suits, proceedings, judgments, rulings or orders by or before any court or any governmental authority that materially adversely affect its ability to perform this Agreement or the rights of the other Party under this Agreement;

  (b) it is a duly organized and validly existing entity of the type described in the introduction to this Agreement and (if applicable) is in good standing under the laws of the jurisdiction of its formation; it has the legal right, power, and authority and is qualified to conduct its business, and to execute and deliver this Agreement and perform its obligations under this Agreement; and all regulatory authorizations have been maintained as necessary for it to perform legally its obligations under this Agreement;

  (c) its making and performing this Agreement are within its powers, have been duly authorized by all necessary action on its part, and do not and will not violate any provision of law or any rule, regulation, order, writ, judgment, decree, or other determination presently in effect applicable to it or its governing documents;

  (d) this Agreement constitutes its legal, valid, and binding act and obligation, enforceable against it in accordance with this Agreement’s terms, subject to applicable bankruptcy, insolvency, reorganization and other laws affecting creditor’s rights generally and to general equitable principles;

  (e) there are no bankruptcy, insolvency, reorganization, receivership, or other arrangement proceedings pending or being contemplated by it, or to its knowledge threatened against it; and

  (f) it is entering into this Agreement and the transactions it contemplates in the ordinary course of its business as a generator, transmitter, and/or seller of electricity and, in the case of Buyer, is purchasing the energy for resale.


ARTICLE 6
DEFAULTS AND REMEDIES

 6.1 Early Termination.  (a)  If a Triggering Event (as defined in Section 6.2 or 6.3) occurs with respect to Seller or Buyer (the “Affected Party”) and at the time some OPAs remain exercisable or the Seasonal Delivery Periods for all Exercised OPAs have not expired, the other Party (the “Notifying Party”) may:

   (i) by notice to the Affected Party on or before the 60th Day following the Notifying Party’s discovery of the Triggering Event, establish a date (which may be no earlier than the 10th Business Day after the notice or the 10th Business Day following the Notifying Party’s discovery of the Triggering Event, whichever is later) on which the Parties’ rights and obligations to exercise and honor OPAs and to sell, deliver, purchase, and receive energy under this Agreement will terminate (“Early Termination Date”); provided, however, that an Early Termination Date automatically shall be deemed to have occurred on the occurrence of any Triggering Event listed in Section 6.2(d) or 6.3(d);

  (ii) withhold any payments due in respect of this Agreement; and/or

  (iii) suspend scheduling or deliveries of energy.

 (b) In connection with any Early Termination Date, the Notifying Party shall calculate the “Termination Payment” due to it from the Affected Party, which shall equal:

  (i) if Seller is the Notifying Party:

   (A) the sum, for all Exercised OPAs, of the excess (if any) of:

    (x) the unpaid Demand Charges (whether then due or due in the future) for the applicable Exercised OPA,

  over

    (y) the demand charges either quoted by a bona fide third-party offer or that Seller reasonably expects to be available in the market as of the Early Termination Date for the demand charges under replacement contracts for sales of firm seasonal peaking energy on substantially the same terms as the applicable Exercised OPA,

  in each case discounted from the date payment otherwise would be due to the Early Termination Date at a rate per annum equal to the average yield to maturity of United States Treasury obligations having a comparable maturity date,

  plus

    (B) the reasonable associated costs and attorneys’ fees; or

  (ii) if Buyer is the Notifying Party:

   (A) the option premium either quoted by a bona fide third-party offer or that Buyer reasonably expects to be available in the market as of the Early Termination Date for replacement options on substantially the same terms as all OPAs that, as of the Early Termination Date, are not Exercised OPAs,

 plus

   (B) the sum, for all Exercised OPAs, of the excess (if any) of:

    (x) the demand charges either quoted by a bona fide third-party offer or that Buyer reasonably expects to be available in the market as of the Early Termination Date for the demand charges under replacement contracts for purchases of firm seasonal peaking energy on substantially the same terms as the applicable Exercised OPA,

  over

    (y) the unpaid Demand Charges (whether then due or due in the future) for the applicable Exercised OPA,

  in each case discounted as provided in Section 6.1(b)(i)(A),

  plus

    (C) the reasonable associated costs and attorneys’ fees. 

 (c) To ascertain the market prices of replacement options and contracts, the Notifying Party may consider, among other valuations, quotations from leading dealers in energy purchase and sale contracts and other bona fide third-party offers, all adjusted for the duration of the remaining term and any differential based on applicable transmission charges.  If the Notifying Party is unable to calculate an amount under Section 6.1(b)(i)(A)(y) or 6.1(b)(ii)(B)(x), whichever is applicable, because demand charges are not reasonably obtainable in the market as of the Early Termination Date, the Notifying Party instead shall calculate the amount under that clause using another methodology approved by the Affected Party (which approval shall not be withheld unreasonably) designed to represent what demand charges would have been had replacement contracts providing for demand charges been obtainable.

 (d) The Notifying Party shall notify the Affected Party of the amount of the Termination Payment, inclusive of a statement showing its determination of that amount.  The Affected Party shall pay the entire Termination Payment to the Notifying Party on or before the 10th Day following the notice specifying the Termination Payment.  At the time for payment of any amount due under this Section 6.1, each Party shall pay to the other Party all additional amounts it then is obligated to pay the other Party under this Agreement, but all such amounts shall be netted and aggregated with any Termination Payment.  If the Affected Party disagrees with the calculation of the Termination Payment, the issue shall be submitted to arbitration under Article 11, and the resulting Termination Payment shall be due and payable on the 10th Business Day after the determination.

 6.2 Seller Triggering Event.  As used in this Agreement, the term “Triggering Event” means with respect to Seller:

  (a) the failure by Seller to make, when due, any payment required under this Agreement if that failure is not remedied on or before the 10th Business Day after Buyer notifies Seller of the failure, unless payment is the subject of a good-faith dispute as described in Section 8.2(c);

  (b) any representation or warranty made by Seller in this Agreement shall prove to have been false or misleading in any material respect when made or deemed to be repeated;

  (c) the failure by Seller to perform any covenant set forth in this Agreement (other than its obligations to sell or deliver energy or to make any payments or its obligations that are otherwise specifically covered in this Section 6.2 as a separate Triggering Event), which failure has a material and adverse effect on Buyer, and the failure is not excused under Article 10 or cured on or before the 10th Business Day after Buyer notifies Seller of the failure;

  (d) Seller’s Guarantor [or Seller if the guaranty by Seller’s Guarantor does not apply] or its successor:

   (i) makes an assignment or any general arrangement for the benefit of creditors,

   (ii) files a petition or otherwise commences, authorizes, or acquiesces in the commencement of a proceeding or cause under any bankruptcy or similar law for the protection of creditors, or such a petition is filed against it and such proceeding remains undismissed for 30 days,

   (iii) otherwise becomes bankrupt or insolvent (however evidenced), or

   (iv) is unable to pay its debts as they fall due;

  (e) if Seller’s Guarantor [or Seller if the guaranty by Seller’s Guarantor does not apply] or its successor then has long-term unsecured debt not supported by third-party credit enhancement that is rated by Standard & Poors and Moody’s Investors Service and that debt is rated below BBB- by Standard & Poors and below Baa3 by Moody’s Investors Service, on or before the second Business Day following notice from Buyer to Seller, Seller fails to deposit with an escrow agent reasonably acceptable to Buyer, on terms reasonably acceptable to Buyer, cash or cash equivalents in an amount equal to the Termination Payment that would be due if an Early Termination Date occurred on the date of the notice (but not to exceed as of any time $300,000 multiplied by the sum of the number of OPAs as of that time (i) the Strike Date for which have not yet occurred and (ii) that are Exercised OPAs and the Seasonal Delivery Period for which have not yet expired, but counting each such OPA only once) or, following further notice from Buyer to Seller (which notice may be given no sooner than the 90th Day following the most recent preceding notice under this Section 6.2(e)), Seller fails to increase that amount to the extent necessary to cause the amount escrowed to continue to equal to the Termination Payment that would be due if an Early Termination Date occurred on the date of that notice, and limited as described above (it being understood that amounts so escrowed shall be released to Seller if the rating of Seller’s Guarantor’s debt returns to the minimum levels listed at the beginning of this Section 6.2(e)); or

  (f) Seller’s Guarantor [or Seller if the guaranty by Seller’s Guarantor does not apply] or its successor defaults on its indebtedness to third parties resulting in an acceleration of obligations of Seller’s Guarantor [or Seller if the guaranty by Seller’s Guarantor does not apply] in excess of $50,000,000.

 6.3 Buyer Triggering Event.  As used in this Agreement, the term “Triggering Event” means with respect to Buyer:

  (a) the failure by Buyer to make, when due, any payment required under this Agreement if that failure is not remedied on or before the 10th Business Day after Seller notifies Buyer of the failure, unless payment is the subject of a good-faith dispute as described in Section 8.2(c);

  (b) any representation or warranty made by Buyer in this Agreement shall prove to have been false or misleading in any material respect when made or deemed to be repeated;

  (c) the failure by Buyer to perform any covenant set forth in this Agreement (other than its obligations to take delivery of energy or to make any payments or its obligations that are otherwise specifically covered in this Section 6.3 as a separate Triggering Event), which failure has a material and adverse effect on Seller, and the failure is not excused under Article 10 or cured on or before the 10th Business Day after Seller notifies Buyer of the failure;

  (d) Buyer’s Guarantor [or Buyer if there is no Buyer’s Guarantor]:

   (i) makes an assignment or any general arrangement for the benefit of creditors,

   (ii) files a petition or otherwise commences, authorizes, or acquiesces in the commencement of a proceeding or cause under any bankruptcy or similar law for the protection of creditors, or such a petition is filed against it and such proceeding remains undismissed for 30 days,

   (iii) otherwise becomes bankrupt or insolvent (however evidenced), or

   (iv) is unable to pay its debts as they fall due;

  (e) if Buyer’s Guarantor [or Buyer if there is no Buyer’s Guarantor] then has long-term unsecured debt not supported by third-party credit enhancement that is rated by Standard & Poors and Moody’s Investors Service and that debt is rated below BBB- by Standard & Poors and below Baa3 by Moody’s Investors Service, on or before the second Business Day following notice from Seller to Buyer, Buyer fails to deposit with an escrow agent reasonably acceptable to Seller, on terms reasonably acceptable to Seller, cash or cash equivalents in an amount equal to the Termination Payment that would be due if an Early Termination Date occurred on the date of the notice (but not to exceed as of any time $175,000 multiplied by the number of OPAs as of that time that are Exercised OPAs and the Seasonal Delivery Period for which have not yet expired) or, following further notice from Seller to Buyer (which notice may be given no sooner than the 90th Day following the most recent preceding notice under this Section 6.3(e)), Buyer fails to increase that amount to the extent necessary to cause the amount escrowed to continue to equal to the Termination Payment that would be due if an Early Termination Date occurred on the date of that notice, and limited as described above (it being understood that amounts so escrowed shall be released to Buyer if the rating of its debt returns to the minimum levels listed at the beginning of this Section 6.3(e)); or

  (f) Buyer’s Guarantor [or Buyer if there is no Buyer’s Guarantor] defaults on its indebtedness to third parties resulting in an acceleration of obligations of Buyer’s Guarantor [or Buyer if there is no Buyer’s Guarantor] in excess of $50,000,000.

 6.4 Termination of Individual OPAs for Unreliability.  (a) As used in this Agreement:

  (i) “Deficiency Period” means a continuous period of more than 60 minutes:

   (A) that occurs during a Scheduled Period, other than a Scheduled Period occurring during the first week of either of the first two Seasonal Delivery Periods for which there are Exercised OPAs,

   (B) during which Seller fails to deliver energy at the rate at which energy is scheduled to be delivered in accordance with Section 2.2, and

   (C) Seller’s failure is not excused under Article 10, an Agreed Interruption, caused by Buyer, or a Seller Firm Temporary Interruption.

  (ii) “Deficiency Day” means a Day during which a Deficiency Period occurs.

 (b)  If  the following circumstances have occurred:

  (i) either (A) a total of 10 or more Deficiency Days (regardless of whether consecutive) have occurred during the current Seasonal Delivery Period and the immediately preceding Seasonal Delivery Period, with three or more of those Deficiency Days occurring during each of those two Seasonal Delivery Periods or (B) a total of 15 or more Deficiency Days (regardless of whether consecutive) have occurred during the current Seasonal Delivery Period, and

  (ii) the aggregate quantity of energy that has been scheduled for delivery in accordance with Section 2.2 but that Seller has failed to deliver (other than failures that are excused under Article 10, are Agreed Interruptions, are caused by Buyer, or are Seller Firm Temporary Interruptions) since the first scheduling of energy under this Agreement exceeds 2% of the aggregate quantity of energy so scheduled,

then Buyer, by notice to Seller on or before the 30th day following the latest such Deficiency Day to have occurred, may cancel as of a date specified in the notice (which may be no earlier than the 10th Business Day following the date of the notice) a whole number of OPAs for that Seasonal Delivery Period and each future Seasonal Delivery Period that Buyer shall specify in the notice, which number, when added to the number of OPAs canceled in all prior notices under Section 6.4(b) for that Seasonal Delivery Period, may not exceed the number calculated in accordance with Section 6.4(c).

 (c) The number of OPAs canceled under Section 6.4(b) for any Seasonal Delivery Period may not exceed the result of the following equation:

QD          1  
───   ×   ───
T           R1

where:

 QD = the aggregate quantity of energy (expressed in MWH) that was scheduled for delivery in accordance with Section 2.2 during the Deficiency Periods that have occurred during the current Seasonal Delivery Period and (if the cancellation is to occur under Section 6.4(b)(i)(A)) the immediately preceding Seasonal Delivery Period but that Seller failed to deliver during those Deficiency Periods (except to the extent that the failure is excused under Article 10, is an Agreed Interruption, is caused by Buyer, or is a Seller Firm Temporary Interruption), calculated using the integral of the instantaneous rates of delivery over each such period

 T = the aggregate duration (expressed in hours or fractions of an hour) of the Deficiency Periods during the current Seasonal Delivery Period and (if the cancellation is to occur under Section 6.4(b)(i)(A)) the immediately preceding Seasonal Delivery Period

 R1 = the OPA Quantity for a single OPA (i.e., 25 MW)

 (d) Buyer may send more than one notice under Section 6.4(b) provided that, at the time each such notice is sent, it is entitled by application of Section 6.4(c) to cancel one or more additional OPAs.

 (e) On any such cancellation:

  (i) Seller shall pay Buyer an amount equal to the Termination Payment that would have been due from Seller under Section 6.1(b)(ii) had the OPAs so terminated been the only OPAs, with the amount of the payment calculated and paid as provided in Section 6.1 for a Termination Payment, and
  (ii) the number of Exercised OPAs for that Seasonal Delivery Period and each future Seasonal Delivery Period shall be reduced by the number of OPAs then cancelled (and, if in a future Seasonal Delivery Period the total number of OPAs to be cancelled exceeds the number of Exercised OPAs, the number of OPAs in that future Seasonal Delivery Period that are not Exercised OPAs shall be reduced by the amount of that excess).

 6.5 Other Events.  If a Party is regulated by a federal, state, or local regulatory body, and that body disallows all or any portion of any costs incurred or yet to be incurred by that Party under any provision of this Agreement, that action shall not operate to excuse that Party from performance of any obligation, nor shall that action give rise to any right of either Party to any refund or retroactive adjustment of the amounts payable under this Agreement.  Notwithstanding the foregoing, if a Party’s activities under this Agreement become subject to regulation of any kind whatsoever under any law to a greater or different extent than that existing on the date of this Agreement and the new law or regulation either (a) renders this Agreement illegal or unenforceable or (b) materially and adversely affects the business of that Party, with respect to its financial position or otherwise, then in the case of (a) above, either Party, and in the case of (b) above, only the Party so materially and adversely affected, may declare an Early Termination Date, whereupon the provisions of Section 6.1 shall apply with the other Party being deemed to be the Notifying Party under Section 6.1.

 6.6 Offset.  Each Party reserves to itself all rights, set-offs, counterclaims and other remedies and defenses consistent with Article 3 (to the extent not expressly waived or denied in this Agreement) that it has or to which it may be entitled arising from or out of this Agreement.  All obligations to make payment in connection with or under this Agreement may be offset against each other, set off, or recouped from those other payments.


ARTICLE 7
CONTROL AREA SERVICES AND OPERATIONS

 7.1 Control Area Services.  Unless both Parties otherwise specifically agree in a writing, Seller, at its sole cost and expense, shall provide or cause to be provided all control area services necessary or appropriate to effectuate all deliveries and receipts under this Agreement up to the Scheduled Delivery Points, and Buyer, at its sole cost and expense, shall provide or cause to be provided all such services at and after the Scheduled Delivery Points.  The control area services shall be provided consistent with applicable North American Electric Reliability Council (“NERC”) requirements and guidelines, applicable NERC regional area requirements and guidelines, and applicable guidelines and requirements of any power pool affected by or involved in the transaction.

 7.2 Operating Procedures.  Seller and Buyer shall develop jointly written operating procedures no later than the 90th Day prior to the beginning of the first Seasonal Delivery Period of an Exercised OPA to begin. Topics covered by the operating procedures shall include, but not be limited to, methods of day-to-day communications, and any key personnel lists for Seller and Buyer.  Seller and Buyer also shall establish an Operating Committee, consisting of two representatives of each Party, which shall meet periodically to oversee and coordinate operational issues arising out of this Agreement and the operating procedures.

ARTICLE 8
BILLING AND PAYMENT

 8.1 Amount Owed.  Subject to the provisions of Section 6.6, to the extent applicable, Buyer shall pay Seller, for each calendar month that includes all or part of a Seasonal Delivery Period for an Exercised OPA, an amount equal to the sum of:

  (a) the Demand Charge for that Seasonal Delivery Period multiplied by the sum of the OPA Quantities for all OPAs that are Exercised OPAs for that Seasonal Delivery Period (prorated based on a 30-Day month if only part of the month is within the Seasonal Delivery Period and subject to reduction as provided in Section 3.2(c)),

plus

  (b) the amount of energy delivered to Buyer under this Agreement during that month multiplied by the Energy Charge for OPAs for that Seasonal Delivery Period,

plus

  (c) any supplemental charges for that month incurred as provided in Section 4.1(d).

 8.2 Invoicing and Payment.  (a) The Party owed any amount due under this Agreement (the “Billing Party”) shall submit to the other party (the “Billed Party”) an invoice, in writing, as soon as practicable after the end of each calendar month for energy delivered under this Agreement or for other amounts owed by one Party to the other.  Subject to the remaining provisions of this Section 8.2, the Billed Party shall pay the invoice on or before the 20th Day after receipt.

 (b) If the Billed Party fails to pay the amount of the Billing Party’s invoice when due, then the Billed Party also shall pay interest, at a rate per annum, for each calendar month during which the amount remains unpaid, equal to 2% over the “prime rate” appearing in The Wall Street Journal (or if a range is quoted, the midpoint of that range) on the last Business Day during the preceding calendar month (or if lower, the maximum rate of interest permitted by applicable law), on the amount that is not paid from the date that the amount was due to the date on which the Billing Party receives payment.

 (c) The Billed Party must notify the Billing Party of any portion of the invoice that it disputes on or before the 60th day following delivery of the invoice.  If payment has not been made, the Billed Party may withhold payment of the disputed portion but nonetheless shall pay the undisputed portion when due.  The Billed Party shall pay any disputed amount that subsequently is determined to be owed, and the Billing Party shall refund any amount paid that subsequently is determined not to have been owed, in each case promptly after that determination, with interest at the rate specified in Section 8.2(b) from the date that amount would have been due in the absence of any dispute.

 (d) Billing and payment for quantities of energy delivered under this Agreement shall be based on the quantities scheduled in accordance with Section 2.2, subject to adjustments as provided in this Agreement.  Each Party shall make available to the other Party, on request, statements from control area operators from which energy was scheduled for delivery evidencing the quantities of energy actually delivered.  Each Party shall use commercially reasonable efforts to obtain information from the applicable control area operators and to make that information available as soon as practicable.  Should control area statements, or logs or other records maintained by the Parties, not agree as to the quantity of energy actually delivered, the Parties promptly shall seek resolution from the control area operators and between themselves to determine the quantity of energy actually delivered.  Disagreements in records of this nature may form the basis for Buyer to dispute an invoice in accordance with the procedures, and subject to the consequences, described in Section 8.2(c).

 (e) The Billing Party shall permit independent auditors selected and paid for by the Billed Party access during normal working hours to the records of the Billing Party as necessary to verify the accuracy of any invoice, statement, or other document relating to this Agreement, provided the auditors agree in writing to keep information examined confidential except to the extent appropriate to disclose to the Billed Party discrepancies in the amounts invoiced.  If any resulting dispute is resolved through arbitration under Article 11, the Billing Party shall pay the reasonable costs and fees of the Billed Party’s auditor if the Billed Party prevails in that arbitration.


ARTICLE 9
ASSIGNMENT

 9.1 Assignment of Rights.  Either Party, without the need for consent from the other Party (but without relieving itself from liability under this Agreement), may:

  (a) transfer, pledge, or assign its rights under this Agreement as security for any financing,

  (b) transfer or assign its rights under this Agreement to an affiliate of that Party, or

  (c) transfer or assign its rights under this Agreement to any person or entity succeeding to all or substantially all of the assets of that Party, but only if the assignee executes and delivers to the other Party a written instrument under which it agrees to be bound by the provisions of this Agreement in all respects and represents and warrants that the representations and warranties in Article 5 are true with respect to it as of the effective date of the transfer and assignment.

 9.2 Assignment Generally.  This Agreement shall be binding on and inure to the benefit of the Parties and their respective successors and permitted assigns.  Except as provided in this Article 9, a Party may assign its rights under this Agreement only if the other Party agrees, which agreement may not be withheld unreasonably.  The provisions of this Agreement shall not impart rights enforceable by any person, firm, or organization not a Party or not bound as a Party, or not a permitted successor or assignee of a Party bound by this Agreement. 


ARTICLE 10
FORCE MAJEURE 

 10.1 Excuse for Force Majeure.  (a) If either Party is rendered unable, by an event of force majeure, to carry out wholly or in part its obligations under this Agreement, and if that Party notifies the other of the existence and the full particulars of the event of force majeure as soon as practicable after the occurrence of the cause relied on, then the obligations of the Party affected by that event of force majeure (other than the obligation to make payments under this Agreement), shall be suspended from the inception and throughout the period of continuance of the inability so caused, but for no longer period.  The Party affected shall endeavor to remedy the event of force majeure with all reasonable dispatch.  Further, if either Party is rendered unable, by an event of force majeure, to carry out its scheduling and delivery obligations, then that Party shall also be excused from its deficiency payment obligations as set forth in Article 3 under the same terms and conditions as described in the two preceding sentences; provided, however, that if Buyer declares force majeure under this sentence it shall provide Seller with the opportunity to change Scheduled Delivery Point(s) such that the impact of the force majeure condition is mitigated or eliminated.  As used in this Agreement, the term “force majeure” means causes of the kind not reasonably within the control of the Party claiming suspension and that, by the exercise of due diligence, the Party could not have prevented or is unable to overcome.  These causes include the following and other causes of a similar nature:  acts of public enemies, earthquake, flood, explosion, fire, lightning, severe storms, and government orders preventing performance.

 (b) An event of force majeure that affects the supply or transmission of energy constitutes force majeure, but only until Seller obtains or, using commercially reasonable efforts, could obtain alternate sources of supply and related transmission to remedy the force majeure.  If Seller suffers an interruption in its generation supply or related transmission due to an event constituting force majeure that affects a source of generation or transmission that Seller was utilizing to fulfill its obligations under this Agreement but a commercially reasonable period for replacing the affected source or transmission as just provided has lapsed, any continued interruption of delivery of energy no longer shall be excused to the extent that the sources of generation and transmission still available to Seller could be used in a commercially reasonable manner to deliver energy to Buyer.  Buyer’s entry into new arrangements to purchase energy from third parties outside its system to replace energy that Seller fails to deliver when Seller claims force majeure is evidence that force majeure no longer exists.

 (c) If a Party affected by an event of force majeure must decide which of several obligations that it has to deliver or take delivery of energy would be affected or would be restored, it shall endeavor in good faith, taking into account all its obligations to third parties (including the other Party), to interrupt nonfirm obligations to deliver or take delivery of energy before it interrupts any firm obligations, including those under this Agreement, to restore all obligations under this Agreement before restoring any nonfirm obligations and to interrupt and restore all such firm obligations pro rata.
 (d) If the delivery or receipt of energy is excused under this Section 10.1, that quantity of energy shall not be counted as having been scheduled for purposes of the proviso at the end of Section 2.2(a).

 10.2 Records.  Each Party shall retain records of events it claims under Section 10.1 as force majeure and of its efforts to remedy the effects of the force majeure and, on request, shall make those records available to the other Party.


ARTICLE 11
ARBITRATION

 11.1 Submission to Arbitration.  Any dispute or need for interpretation arising out of the provisions of this Agreement that specifically require resolution by arbitration shall be submitted to binding arbitration by one arbitrator and who has not previously been employed by either Party, and does not have a direct or indirect interest in either Party or the subject matter of the arbitration and has expertise in matters affecting electrical markets.  The arbitrator shall either be as mutually agreed by the Parties on or before the 30th Day after notice from either Party requesting arbitration, or failing agreement, shall be selected under the expedited rules of the American Arbitration Association (the “AAA”).

 11.2 Conduct of Arbitration.  The arbitration shall be held in alternating locations of the home offices of Seller and Buyer, commencing with Seller’s office.  The rules of the AAA shall apply to the extent not inconsistent with this Agreement.  The arbitration shall be conducted as follows:

  (a) not later than the seventh Day before the hearing date set by the arbitrator each Party shall submit a brief supporting its position and, if an amount allegedly owing is in dispute, setting a single dollar figure for settlement,

  (b) the hearing shall be conducted on a confidential basis,

  (c) each Party shall divide equally the cost of the hearing and each shall be responsible for its own expenses and those of its counsel and representatives, and

  (d) evidence concerning the financial position or organizational make-up of the Parties, any offer made or the details of any negotiation prior to arbitration and the cost to the Parties of their representatives and counsel shall not be permissible.

 11.3 Determination.  The arbitrator shall notify the Parties of its determination, which must be the position submitted by one of the Parties under Section 11.2(a).  The determination shall be final and binding on both Parties, and either Party may enforce the determination by judicial proceedings not inconsistent with this Agreement.


ARTICLE 12
TAXES

 12.1 General.  Each of Buyer and Seller shall use reasonable efforts to implement the provisions of and to administer this Agreement in accordance with their intent to minimize taxes, so long as neither Party is materially adversely affected by those efforts.  A Party, on notice from the other, shall provide a certificate of exemption or other reasonably satisfactory evidence of exemption if either Party is exempt from taxes, and shall use reasonable efforts to obtain and cooperate with obtaining any exemption from or reduction of tax.  A Party with knowledge of a tax on the purchase or sale of energy that may be applicable to the energy sold under this Agreement shall notify the other Party, in advance, of the applicability of such tax and shall also notify the other Party of any proposal to implement a new tax or apply an existing tax to the purchase, sale, delivery, or receipt of energy under this Agreement.

 12.2 Applicable Taxes.  Buyer shall pay all existing and any new sales, use, excise, ad valorem, and any other similar taxes, if any, imposed or levied by any governmental agency on the energy sold and delivered under this Agreement arising at or after the Scheduled Delivery Points.  Seller shall likewise be responsible for any such taxes prior to delivery of energy to Buyer to the Scheduled Delivery Points.  Buyer shall indemnify, defend, and hold Seller harmless from any liability for all such taxes for which Buyer is responsible.  Seller shall indemnify, defend, and  hold Buyer harmless from any liability from all such taxes for which Seller is responsible.  Buyer shall reimburse Seller promptly on demand for the amount of any such tax that is Buyer’s responsibility that Seller remits, plus any penalties and interest incurred and remitted, except for penalties resulting from Seller’s conduct.  Likewise, Seller shall reimburse Buyer promptly on demand for the amount of any such tax that is Seller’s responsibility herewith that Buyer remits, plus any penalties, interest incurred and remitted, except penalties as a result from Buyer’s conduct.


